body:
Did some more debugging.

I assume `AnimationPlayer` processes the animations via engine internally somehow triggering `AnimationPlayer::advance(..)`. This call then leads to `AnimationMixer::_process_animation(..)`. Or something similar, the latter is the relevant method anyway.

Here, a few method calls happen. Out of these, the interesting one seems to be [`_blend_process`](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/cb3af5afff15669a684ba4f04b9aa5c70dfd09ad/scene/animation/animation_mixer.cpp#L1164), which according to a comment in code:
> Apply value/transform/blend/bezier blends to track caches **and execute** method/audio/**animation tracks**.

Within this somewhat beast of a method, we have this snippet ([here](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/cb3af5afff15669a684ba4f04b9aa5c70dfd09ad/scene/animation/animation_mixer.cpp#L1827-L1839)):
```cpp
StringName anim_name = a->animation_track_get_key_animation(i, idx);
if (String(anim_name) == "[stop]" || !player2->has_animation(anim_name)) {
	// *snip*, just stops the animation, not interesting for this case
} else {
	player2->play(anim_name);
	t->playing = true;
	playing_caches.insert(t);
}
```
The animation track playback is started, but remembering that GDScript [documentation](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_animationplayer.html#class-animationplayer-method-play) for `AnimationPlayer.play` states:
> **Note:** The animation will be updated the next time the **AnimationPlayer** is processed. If other variables are updated at the same time this is called, they may be updated too early. To perform the update immediately, call `advance(0)`.

...this leads me to believe that the targeted `player2` may not be immediately processed during the current process/tick (sorry for my lack of correct terminology here, I'm still quite new to Godot's internals). That is, any keyframes from the start of the animation will not take effect until the next frame. At glance, I could not find anything that immediately processes animations for the `player2` from within this method or seemingly relevant method calls around the `->play(..)` invocation.

Not sure how sound this theory is, as the texture is still changed immediately (the texture changes, but the keyframes are not applied).

Then, I dug a bit more into the internals of `AnimationPlayer` and `AnimationMixer`, stepping into the call `player2->play(anim_name)` in the above snippet. There, a few method calls in, execution ends up within `AnimationPlayer::play_section`, where I noticed that the `current_animation_changed` signal is emited instantly during the `player2->play(anim_name)` call ([here](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/cb3af5afff15669a684ba4f04b9aa5c70dfd09ad/scene/animation/animation_player.cpp#L514)).

With this knowledge, to further test theory, before diving engine code for modifications, I attached the following script to the `CharacterAnimations` in the MRP:

```
@tool
extends AnimationPlayer

func _on_current_animation_changed(_name: String) -> void:
	advance(0)
```
Then, I connected this to the `current_animation_changed` signal on the `CharacterAnimations`, with the connection being **deferred**:

<img width="839" height="346" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/d55e6e44-1b87-43d3-b137-e6104f173348" />

The thinking here being: when animation playback track hits the frame when the animation should change, the animation is changed and `current_animation_changed` is emitted. The `player2` is not advanced automatically, but we can listen on `current_animation_changed` signal on `player2` to force it to advance immediately.

With this setup, the single frame delay is gone, even when animation player's `callback_mode_process` is set to `ANIMATION_CALLBACK_MODE_PROCESS_IDLE` (the default).

For whatever reason, calling `advance(0)` with non-deferred signal callback does not work. I guess the signal is emitted before the animation change has fully been processed, so advancing it does not apply the expected results. Or something.

Next, I'm probably going to try simply adding `player2->advance(0);` immediately after the `player2->play(..)` call, to see if this fixes the issue, before digging deeper if/where the `player2->play(..)` call attempts to apply the first keyframes. It might be expected, that the `player2` is ticked/advanced after the animation player being processed, but having scene tree in a certain order causes it to be ticked first, so that the first `player2->advance(..)` won't happen until the next frame, when it is a single frame too late. That is, I think this *\*could\** be an update order issue, but I'm not sure which of the animation players is expected to be advanced first in the scene hierarchy within the MRP, so I'll need to take a closer look at how animation players are actually advanced.

Ha! Tried out this little change here (checked out at cb3af5afff):
```patch
diff --git i/scene/animation/animation_mixer.cpp w/scene/animation/animation_mixer.cpp
index 27fb0f4cce..a60f9670cb 100644
--- i/scene/animation/animation_mixer.cpp
+++ w/scene/animation/animation_mixer.cpp
@@ -1834,6 +1834,7 @@ void AnimationMixer::_blend_process(double p_delta, bool p_update_only) {
                                                                }
                                                        } else {
                                                                player2->play(anim_name);
+                                                               player2->advance(0);
                                                                t->playing = true;
                                                                playing_caches.insert(t);
                                                        }
```
...and surely enough, the issue is gone.

**I don't feel this is a good/robust solution, though,** as just advancing the target animation player _while another animation player is still in progress of processing its own animation_ feels terribly hacky. Or at least it feels like quite a nuclear solution to kick off another animation processing tick from within `_process_animation`. Also, without taking a deeper dive at the code, this sounds like an infinite loop just waiting to happen.

I haven't yet really dug into what `->play(..)` or `->advance(..)` do under the hood, but I bet there is some nicer approach for forcing the `player2` to apply the keyframes for the first frame during the same tick when `->play(..)` is invoked.

